{"class":100},[79,1384,115],{"class":96},[79,1386,168],{"class":100},[14,1388,1389],{},"Self-joins are the classic way to walk a hierarchy (employees→managers). For deep hierarchies, use recursive CTEs (chapter 19).",[18,1391,1393],{"id":1392},"join-algorithms-what-the-planner-chooses","Join Algorithms (what the planner chooses)",[14,1395,1396,1397,1400],{},"The optimizer picks one of these per join — visible in ",[76,1398,1399],{},"EXPLAIN",":",[1402,1403,1404,1420],"table",{},[1405,1406,1407],"thead",{},[1408,1409,1410,1414,1417],"tr",{},[1411,1412,1413],"th",{},"Algorithm",[1411,1415,1416],{},"Best when",[1411,1418,1419],{},"Cost",[1421,1422,1423,1437,1450],"tbody",{},[1408,1424,1425,1431,1434],{},[1426,1427,1428],"td",{},[26,1429,1430],{},"Nested Loop",[1426,1432,1433],{},"One side is small, or indexed lookup on the inner.",[1426,1435,1436],{},"O(N × M) worst, O(N × log M) with index.",[1408,1438,1439,1444,1447],{},[1426,1440,1441],{},[26,1442,1443],{},"Hash Join",[1426,1445,1446],{},"Both sides large, no usable index, equality condition.",[1426,1448,1449],{},"O(N + M) — builds a hash table on the smaller side.",[1408,1451,1452,1457,1460],{},[1426,1453,1454],{},[26,1455,1456],{},"Merge Join",[1426,1458,1459],{},"Both sides pre-sorted on the join key (e.g., via indexes).",[1426,1461,1462],{},"O(N + M) — both inputs must be sorted.",[14,1464,1465],{},"You don't choose the algorithm — the planner does, based on table stats. But knowing which it picked helps diagnose slow joins. See chapter 26 (EXPLAIN).",[18,1467,1469],{"id":1468},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[1471,1472,1473,1491,1508,1527,1551],"ul",{},[34,1474,1475,1478,1479,1482,1483,1486,1487,1490],{},[26,1476,1477],{},"Idiom",": use ",[76,1480,1481],{},"LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL"," (anti-join) instead of ",[76,1484,1485],{},"NOT IN (subquery)"," — the anti-join is immune to the NULL-poisoning trap that breaks ",[76,1488,1489],{},"NOT IN",", and it's usually faster because the planner can use a hash anti-join instead of materializing the subquery.",[34,1492,1493,1496,1497,1500,1501,1503,1504,1507],{},[26,1494,1495],{},"Performance",": a hash join needs memory to build the hash table (the smaller input). If ",[76,1498,1499],{},"work_mem"," is too low, PostgreSQL spills the hash to disk and performance collapses — increase ",[76,1502,1499],{}," for big join queries (set it per-session: ",[76,1505,1506],{},"SET work_mem = '256MB';","), but beware that it's per-node, not per-query, so a 10-node plan with 256MB each can blow up memory.",[34,1509,1510,1512,1513,1515,1516,1518,1519,1522,1523,1526],{},[26,1511,1477],{},": prefer ",[76,1514,1082],{}," over ",[76,1517,1089],{}," when the column names match — it's shorter, returns the joined column once (no need to pick ",[76,1520,1521],{},"a.col"," vs ",[76,1524,1525],{},"b.col","), and signals intent (\"these are the same key\") more clearly than an arbitrary equality.",[34,1528,1529,1532,1533,1536,1537,1539,1540,1542,1543,1546,1547,1550],{},[26,1530,1531],{},"Debug",": when a join returns ",[50,1534,1535],{},"more"," rows than expected, look for a non-unique join key on the \"many\" side — a ",[76,1538,401],{}," from customers to orders on ",[76,1541,165],{}," produces one row per ",[50,1544,1545],{},"order",", not per customer. If you want one row per customer, aggregate (",[76,1548,1549],{},"GROUP BY",") or use a lateral\u002Fdistinct-on subquery.",[34,1552,1553,1556,1557,1101,1559,1561,1562,1564,1565,1567,1568,1570,1571,1573,1574,663,1576,1578],{},[26,1554,1555],{},"Portability",": ",[76,1558,919],{},[76,1560,662],{}," aren't supported in older MySQL (pre-8.0 has no ",[76,1563,662],{},") and SQLite (no ",[76,1566,662],{},") — for portable code, stick to ",[76,1569,148],{}," and emulate ",[76,1572,662],{}," with ",[76,1575,673],{},[76,1577,1111],{}," works everywhere but is dangerous (see above).",[18,1580,1582],{"id":1581},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[1471,1584,1585,1611,1629,1648,1662,1685,1711,1727],{},[34,1586,1587,1556,1594,1597,1598,1600,1601,1604,1605,1607,1608,101],{},[26,1588,1589,670,1591,1593],{},[76,1590,401],{},[76,1592,425],{}," on the right table converts to INNER JOIN",[76,1595,1596],{},"SELECT ... FROM c LEFT JOIN o ON c.id=o.customer_id WHERE o.amount > 100"," drops customers with no orders (the ",[76,1599,425],{}," filters out the NULL-extended rows). To filter right-table rows ",[50,1602,1603],{},"without"," losing left-only rows, put the condition in the ",[76,1606,148],{}," clause: ",[76,1609,1610],{},"LEFT JOIN o ON c.id=o.customer_id AND o.amount > 100",[34,1612,1613,1616,1617,1620,1621,1624,1625,1628],{},[26,1614,1615],{},"Row multiplication",": joining to a \"many\" side multiplies rows. ",[76,1618,1619],{},"LEFT JOIN orders"," on a customer with 5 orders returns 5 rows for that customer — a ",[76,1622,1623],{},"SELECT c.*"," \"to list customers\" unexpectedly has duplicates. Use ",[76,1626,1627],{},"DISTINCT"," or aggregate if you want one row per customer.",[34,1630,1631,1556,1640,1643,1644,1647],{},[26,1632,1633,1636,1637,1639],{},[76,1634,1635],{},"COUNT(*)"," on a ",[76,1638,401],{}," lies",[76,1641,1642],{},"SELECT c.name, COUNT(*) FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON ... GROUP BY c.name"," counts 1 even for customers with no orders (the NULL-extended row still counts). Use ",[76,1645,1646],{},"COUNT(o.id)"," — counting a non-nullable right-column — to count only matched rows (NULLs don't count).",[34,1649,1650,1556,1655,1658,1659,1661],{},[26,1651,1652,1654],{},[76,1653,919],{}," and quoted columns",[76,1656,1657],{},"USING (\"CustomerID\")"," works but is case-sensitive and fragile; prefer snake_case column names to keep ",[76,1660,919],{}," ergonomic.",[34,1663,1664,1556,1667,1670,1671,1673,1674,1677,1678,1681,1682,1684],{},[26,1665,1666],{},"Joining on NULL",[76,1668,1669],{},"NULL = NULL"," is UNKNOWN, so a join ",[76,1672,1089],{}," does ",[26,1675,1676],{},"not"," match rows where both sides are NULL. If NULL-keys should match, use ",[76,1679,1680],{},"IS NOT DISTINCT FROM"," in the ",[76,1683,148],{}," (slower — no index).",[34,1686,1687,1692,1693,1696,1697,1101,1700,1703,1704,1101,1706,107,1708,1710],{},[26,1688,1689,1691],{},[76,1690,1111],{}," surprises",": it joins on ",[50,1694,1695],{},"all"," common columns. If ",[76,1698,1699],{},"customers",[76,1701,1702],{},"orders"," both have ",[76,1705,115],{},[76,1707,1150],{},[76,1709,1111],{}," requires all four to match — almost certainly not what you want.",[34,1712,1713,1556,1716,1719,1720,1722,1723,1726],{},[26,1714,1715],{},"Three-way joins and column ambiguity",[76,1717,1718],{},"SELECT id FROM customers JOIN orders ON ..."," fails — ",[76,1721,115],{}," exists in both tables. Always qualify (",[76,1724,1725],{},"SELECT c.id",") in multi-table queries, even if it happens to be unambiguous today.",[34,1728,1729,1734,1735,1737,1738,1740,1741,1743],{},[26,1730,1731,1733],{},[76,1732,529],{}," readability",": most teams ban ",[76,1736,529],{}," in code style and require rewriting as ",[76,1739,401],{}," — it's easier to read left-to-right and most query authors think \"preserve the first table.\" Reserve ",[76,1742,529],{}," for when rewriting is genuinely awkward.",[18,1745,1747],{"id":1746},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1749,1750,1751,1754],{},"A developer wants a report of ",[26,1752,1753],{},"all customers and the total of their orders",", including customers who haven't ordered yet (showing $0). This query is wrong. Why?",[66,1756,1757],{"language":68},[70,1758,1760],{"className":72,"code":1759,"language":68,"meta":74,"style":74},"SELECT c.name, SUM(o.amount) AS total\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.ordered_on >= '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY c.name;\n",[76,1761,1762,1794,1800,1822,1839],{"__ignoreMap":74},[79,1763,1764,1766,1768,1770,1772,1774,1777,1780,1782,1784,1786,1788,1791],{"class":81,"line":82},[79,1765,93],{"class":92},[79,1767,97],{"class":96},[79,1769,101],{"class":100},[79,1771,104],{"class":96},[79,1773,107],{"class":100},[79,1775,1776],{"class":96},"SUM",[79,1778,1779],{"class":100},"(",[79,1781,110],{"class":96},[79,1783,101],{"class":100},[79,1785,461],{"class":96},[79,1787,239],{"class":100},[79,1789,1790],{"class":92},"AS",[79,1792,1793],{"class":100}," total\n",[79,1795,1796,1798],{"class":81,"line":89},[79,1797,134],{"class":92},[79,1799,137],{"class":100},[79,1801,1802,1804,1806,1808,1810,1812,1814,1816,1818,1820],{"class":81,"line":131},[79,1803,401],{"class":92},[79,1805,145],{"class":100},[79,1807,148],{"class":92},[79,1809,97],{"class":96},[79,1811,101],{"class":100},[79,1813,115],{"class":96},[79,1815,157],{"class":92},[79,1817,160],{"class":96},[79,1819,101],{"class":100},[79,1821,420],{"class":96},[79,1823,1824,1826,1828,1830,1833,1836],{"class":81,"line":140},[79,1825,425],{"class":92},[79,1827,160],{"class":96},[79,1829,101],{"class":100},[79,1831,1832],{"class":96},"ordered_on",[79,1834,1835],{"class":92}," >=",[79,1837,1838],{"class":174}," '2024-01-01'\n",[79,1840,1841,1843,1845,1847,1849],{"class":81,"line":171},[79,1842,1549],{"class":92},[79,1844,97],{"class":96},[79,1846,101],{"class":100},[79,1848,104],{"class":96},[79,1850,168],{"class":100},[1852,1853,1854,1858,1861,1887,1897,1999,2008],"details",{},[1855,1856,1857],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1859,1860],{},"Two bugs:",[31,1862,1863,1881],{},[34,1864,1865,1870,1871,1874,1875,1878,1879,101],{},[26,1866,442,1867,1869],{},[76,1868,425],{}," converts the LEFT JOIN to an INNER JOIN."," ",[76,1872,1873],{},"WHERE o.ordered_on >= '2024-01-01'"," filters out the NULL-extended rows for customers with no orders (because ",[76,1876,1877],{},"NULL >= '2024-01-01'"," is UNKNOWN, not TRUE). Customers who haven't ordered disappear from the result — defeating the purpose of the ",[76,1880,401],{},[34,1882,1883,1886],{},[26,1884,1885],{},"Even for customers with orders, only orders from 2024 are summed"," — which may or may not be the intent, but combined with bug #1, the \"include customers with no orders\" requirement is violated.",[14,1888,1889,1890,1892,1893,1896],{},"The fix: move the right-table filter into the ",[76,1891,148],{}," clause so it filters ",[50,1894,1895],{},"which orders match"," without dropping the customer row:",[70,1898,1900],{"className":72,"code":1899,"language":68,"meta":74,"style":74},"SELECT c.name, COALESCE(SUM(o.amount), 0) AS total\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o\n  ON c.id = o.customer_id\n AND o.ordered_on >= '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY c.name;\n",[76,1901,1902,1941,1947,1954,1973,1987],{"__ignoreMap":74},[79,1903,1904,1906,1908,1910,1912,1914,1917,1919,1921,1923,1925,1927,1929,1932,1935,1937,1939],{"class":81,"line":82},[79,1905,93],{"class":92},[79,1907,97],{"class":96},[79,1909,101],{"class":100},[79,1911,104],{"class":96},[79,1913,107],{"class":100},[79,1915,1916],{"class":96},"COALESCE",[79,1918,1779],{"class":100},[79,1920,1776],{"class":96},[79,1922,1779],{"class":100},[79,1924,110],{"class":96},[79,1926,101],{"class":100},[79,1928,461],{"class":96},[79,1930,1931],{"class":100},"), ",[79,1933,1934],{"class":96},"0",[79,1936,239],{"class":100},[79,1938,1790],{"class":92},[79,1940,1793],{"class":100},[79,1942,1943,1945],{"class":81,"line":89},[79,1944,134],{"class":92},[79,1946,137],{"class":100},[79,1948,1949,1951],{"class":81,"line":131},[79,1950,401],{"class":92},[79,1952,1953],{"class":100}," orders o\n",[79,1955,1956,1959,1961,1963,1965,1967,1969,1971],{"class":81,"line":140},[79,1957,1958],{"class":92},"  ON",[79,1960,97],{"class":96},[79,1962,101],{"class":100},[79,1964,115],{"class":96},[79,1966,157],{"class":92},[79,1968,160],{"class":96},[79,1970,101],{"class":100},[79,1972,420],{"class":96},[79,1974,1975,1977,1979,1981,1983,1985],{"class":81,"line":171},[79,1976,1020],{"class":92},[79,1978,160],{"class":96},[79,1980,101],{"class":100},[79,1982,1832],{"class":96},[79,1984,1835],{"class":92},[79,1986,1838],{"class":174},[79,1988,1989,1991,1993,1995,1997],{"class":81,"line":178},[79,1990,1549],{"class":92},[79,1992,97],{"class":96},[79,1994,101],{"class":100},[79,1996,104],{"class":96},[79,1998,168],{"class":100},[14,2000,2001,2002,2004,2005,2007],{},"::\nNow customers with no (matching) orders still appear, with ",[76,2003,1776],{}," of zero rows = NULL, which ",[76,2006,1916],{}," turns into 0.",[14,2009,2010,2013,2014,2016,2017,2019,2020,2022,2023,2026,2027,2029,2030,101],{},[26,2011,2012],{},"The lesson",": conditions on the right table go in ",[76,2015,148],{}," for a ",[76,2018,401],{}," that should preserve all left rows; conditions in ",[76,2021,425],{}," filter the ",[50,2024,2025],{},"final"," rows and silently demote ",[76,2028,401],{}," to ",[76,2031,57],{},[18,2033,2034],{"id":1855},"Summary",[14,2036,2037,2038,2041,2042,2041,2044,2041,2047,2041,2050,2053,2054,2041,2056,2058,2059,2061,2062,2064,2065,1101,2067,101],{},"You can now combine tables with ",[76,2039,2040],{},"INNER","\u002F",[76,2043,230],{},[76,2045,2046],{},"RIGHT",[76,2048,2049],{},"FULL",[76,2051,2052],{},"CROSS"," joins, choose between ",[76,2055,148],{},[76,2057,919],{},", write self-joins, and avoid the ",[76,2060,401],{},"+",[76,2063,425],{}," trap that demotes outer joins to inner joins. Next: aggregating rows with ",[76,2066,1549],{},[76,2068,2069],{},"HAVING",[2071,2072,2073],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-fo